        "original_id": "28cc55a9-ca68-51a9-9c19-e6f869229391",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Jackson Davis, Francis Key Hunt, Francis Scott Key\n- Places: Loudoun House, Frederick, Maryland, Georgetown, D.C., Washington, D.C.\n- Roles: architect, lawyer, author, amateur poet\n- Topics: The Defence of Fort McHenry, The Star-Spangled Banner\n\nFacts:\n- Francis Scott Key was born on August 1, 1779, and he was an American lawyer, author, and amateur poet from Frederick, Maryland, who later lived in Georgetown, D.C., near Washington, D.C.\n- Francis Scott Key wrote the lyrics for a poem first titled \"The Defence of Fort McHenry,\" which, when set to an old English gentlemen's society tune, became the United States' national anthem, \"The Star-Spangled Banner.\"\n- Francis Scott Key died on January 11, 1843.\n- Loudoun House was designed by New York architect Alexander Jackson Davis.\n- Loudoun House was built in 1851 for Francis Key Hunt, who lived from 1817 to 1879 and was named after his mother's cousin, Francis Scott Key.",
